31: /**
32:  * A chain is a write only builder for composing handlers.
33:  * <p>
34:  * A chain object can't be used to handle requests.
35:  * It can be thought of as a Domain Specific Language (DSL), or API, for constructing a {@code List<Handler>}.
36:  * <p>
37:  * To understand the concept of a all chain, it is important to understand that a {@link Handler} can do one of three things:
38:  * <ol>
39:  * <li>Respond to the request (terminating processing);</li>
40:  * <li>{@link Context#insert(Handler...) Insert handlers} and delegate processing;</li>
41:  * <li>Delegate to the {@link Context#next() next handler}.</li>
42:  * </ol>
43:  * <p>
44:  * Methods like {@link Handlers#chain(ServerConfig, ratpack.func.Action)} take a function that acts on a {@code Chain}, and return a {@link Handler}.
45:  * The returned handler effectively just performs an insert of the handlers added to the chain during the action..
46:  * <p>
47:  * It is very common to use this API to declare the handlers for an application as part of startup via the {@link RatpackServerSpec#handlers(Action)} method.
48:  *
49:  * <h3>Registry</h3>
50:  * <p>
51:  * Chains <i>may</i> be backed by a {@link Registry registry}, depending on how the chain was constructed.
52:  * The {@link RatpackServerSpec#handlers(Action)} method backs the chain instance with the server registry.
53:  * The backing registry can be obtained via {@link #getRegistry()} on the chain instance.
54:  * <p>
55:  * This mechanism allows access to “supporting objects” while building the chain.
56:  * Methods such as {@link #all(Class)} also allow obtaining all implementations from the registry to use.
57:  * This can be useful when using the Guice integration (or similar) to allow all instance to be dependency injected through Guice.
58:  *
59:  * <h3>Adding handlers</h3>
60:  * <p>
61:  * The most basic method of Chain API is the {@link #all(Handler)} method.
62:  * The word “all” represents that all requests reaching this point in the chain will flow through the given handler.
63:  * This is in contrast to methods such as {@link #path(String, Handler)} that will only the request through the given handler if the request path matches.
64:  * <p>
65:  * Methods such as {@link #path(String, Handler)}, {@link #when(Predicate, Action)} etc. are merely more convenient forms of {@link #all(Handler)} and use of the static methods of {@link Handlers}.
66:  * <p>
67:  * For each method that takes a literal {@link Handler}, there exists a variant that takes a {@code Class<? extends Handler>}.
68:  * Such methods obtain an instance of the given handler by asking the chain registry for an instance of the given type.
69:  * This is generally most useful if the chain registry is backed by some kind of dependency injection mechanism (like Google Guice)
70:  * that can construct the handler and inject its dependencies as needed.
71:  *
72:  * <h3><a name="path-binding">Path Binding</a></h3>
73:  * <p>
74:  * Methods such as {@link #get(String, Handler)}, {@link #prefix(String, Action)}, accept a string argument as a request path binding specification.
75:  * These strings can contain symbols that allow {@link ratpack.path.PathTokens} to be captured and for path binding to be dynamic.
76:  * For example, the path string {@code "foo/:val"} will match paths such as {@code "foo/bar"}, {@code "foo/123"} or indeed <code>"foo/<i>«anything»</i>"</code>.
77:  * <p>
78:  * The following table describes the types of symbols that can be used in path strings…
79:  * <table>
80:  *   <caption>Path binding symbols</caption>
81:  *   <tr>
82:  *     <th>Path Type</th>
83:  *     <th>Syntax</th>
84:  *     <th>Example</th>
85:  *   </tr>
86:  *   <tr>
87:  *     <td>Literal</td>
88:  *     <td>{@code foo}</td>
89:  *     <td>{@code "foo"}</td>
90:  *   </tr>
91:  *   <tr>
92:  *     <td>Regular Expression Literal</td>
93:  *     <td><code>::<i>«regex»</i></code></td>
94:  *     <td>{@code "foo/::\d+"}</td>
95:  *   </tr>
96:  *   <tr>
97:  *     <td>Optional Path Token</td>
98:  *     <td><code>:<i>«token-name»</i>?</code></td>
99:  *     <td>{@code "foo/:val?"}</td>
100:  *   </tr>
101:  *   <tr>
102:  *     <td>Mandatory Path Token</td>
103:  *     <td><code>:<i>«token-name»</i></code></td>
104:  *     <td>{@code "foo/:val"}</td>
105:  *   </tr>
106:  *   <tr>
107:  *     <td>Optional Regular Expression Path Token</td>
108:  *     <td><code>:<i>«token-name»</i>?:<i>«regex»</i></code></td>
109:  *     <td>{@code "foo/:val?:\d+"}</td>
110:  *   </tr>
111:  *   <tr>
112:  *     <td>Mandatory Regular Expression Path Token</td>
113:  *     <td><code>:<i>«token-name»</i>:<i>«regex»</i></code></td>
114:  *     <td>{@code "foo/:val:\d+"}</td>
115:  *   </tr>
116:  * </table>
117:  * <p>The following example shows different kinds of binding paths in action.</p>
118:  * <pre class="java">{@code
119:  * import ratpack.test.embed.EmbeddedApp;
120:  * import com.google.common.base.MoreObjects;
121:  * import com.google.common.io.BaseEncoding;
122:  * import java.util.Arrays;
123:  * import java.util.Locale;
124:  * import static org.junit.Assert.*;
125:  *
126:  * public class Example {
127:  *   public static void main(String... args) throws Exception {
128:  *     EmbeddedApp.fromHandlers(c -> c
129:  *       .get("favorites/food", ctx -> ctx.render("pizza")) // Literal
130:  *       .get("favorites/::colou?r", ctx -> ctx.render("blue")) // Regular expression literal
131:  *       .get("optionalToken/:tkn?", ctx -> ctx.render(ctx.getPathTokens().toString())) // Optional path token
132:  *       .get("greeting/:name?", ctx -> // Optional path token with default handling
133:  *         ctx.render("Hello " + MoreObjects.firstNonNull(ctx.getPathTokens().get("name"), "world"))
134:  *       )
135:  *       .get("convert/hex/:tkn", ctx -> // Mandatory path token
136:  *         ctx.render("Hello " + BaseEncoding.base64().encode(ctx.getPathTokens().get("tkn").getBytes("UTF-8")))
137:  *       )
138:  *       .get("pi/:precision?:[\\d]+", ctx -> // Optional regular expression path token
139:  *         ctx.render(String.format(Locale.ENGLISH, "%1." + MoreObjects.firstNonNull(ctx.getPathTokens().get("precision"), "5") + "f", Math.PI))
140:  *       )
141:  *       .get("sum/:num1:[\\d]+/:num2:[\\d]+", ctx -> // Mandatory regular expression path tokens
142:  *         ctx.render(
143:  *           Arrays.asList("num1", "num2")
144:  *           .stream()
145:  *           .map(it -> ctx.getPathTokens().get(it))
146:  *           .mapToInt(Integer::valueOf)
147:  *           .sum() + ""
148:  *         )
149:  *       )
150:  *     ).test(httpClient -> {
151:  *       assertEquals("pizza", httpClient.getText("favorites/food")); // Literal value matched
152:  *       assertEquals("blue", httpClient.getText("favorites/color")); // Regular expression literal matched
153:  *       assertEquals("blue", httpClient.getText("favorites/colour")); // Regular expression literal matched
154:  *       assertEquals("{tkn=val}", httpClient.getText("optionalToken/val")); // Optional path token with value specified
155:  *       assertEquals("{tkn=}", httpClient.getText("optionalToken/")); // Optional path token with trailing slash treated as empty string
156:  *       assertEquals("{}", httpClient.getText("optionalToken")); // Optional path token without trailing slash treated as missing
157:  *       assertEquals("Hello Ratpack", httpClient.getText("greeting/Ratpack")); // Optional path token with value specified
158:  *       assertEquals("Hello world", httpClient.getText("greeting")); // Optional path token with default handling
159:  *       assertEquals("Hello UmF0cGFjaw==", httpClient.getText("convert/hex/Ratpack")); // Mandatory path token
160:  *       assertEquals("3.14159", httpClient.getText("pi")); // Optional regular expression path token with default handling
161:  *       assertEquals("3.14", httpClient.getText("pi/2")); // Optional regular expression path token with value specified
162:  *       assertEquals("3.1415927", httpClient.getText("pi/7")); // Optional regular expression path token with value specified
163:  *       assertEquals("42", httpClient.getText("sum/13/29")); // Mandatory regular expression path tokens
164:  *     });
165:  *   }
166:  * }
167:  * }</pre>
168:  *
169:  * <h3>HTTP Method binding</h3>
170:  * <p>
171:  * Methods such as {@link #get(Handler)}, {@link #post(Handler)} etc. bind based on the HTTP method of the request.
172:  * They are effectively a combination of the use of {@link #path(String, Handler)} and the {@link Context#byMethod(Action)} construct
173:  * to declare that the given path <b>ONLY</b> responds to the specified method.
174:  * <p>
175:  * The following two code snippets are identical:
176:  * <pre class="java">{@code
177:  * import ratpack.test.embed.EmbeddedApp;
178:  * import static org.junit.Assert.*;
179:  *
180:  * public class Example {
181:  *   public static void main(String... args) throws Exception {
182:  *     EmbeddedApp.fromHandlers(c -> c
183:  *       .path("foo", ctx ->
184:  *         ctx.byMethod(m -> m
185:  *           .get(() -> ctx.render("ok"))
186:  *         )
187:  *       )
188:  *     ).test(httpClient -> {
189:  *       assertEquals("ok", httpClient.getText("foo"));
190:  *       assertEquals(405, httpClient.post("foo").getStatusCode());
191:  *     });
192:  *   }
193:  * }
194:  * }</pre>
195:  * <pre class="java">{@code
196:  * import ratpack.test.embed.EmbeddedApp;
197:  * import static org.junit.Assert.*;
198:  *
199:  * public class Example {
200:  *   public static void main(String... args) throws Exception {
201:  *     EmbeddedApp.fromHandlers(c -> c
202:  *       .get("foo", ctx -> ctx.render("ok"))
203:  *     ).test(httpClient -> {
204:  *       assertEquals("ok", httpClient.getText("foo"));
205:  *       assertEquals(405, httpClient.post("foo").getStatusCode());
206:  *     });
207:  *   }
208:  * }
209:  * }</pre>
210:  * <p>
211:  * That is, methods such as {@link #get(String, Handler)}, {@link #get(Handler)} etc. <b>terminate</b> processing with a
212:  * {@code 405} (method not supported) client error if the request path matches but the HTTP method does not.
213:  * They <b>should not be used</b> for URLs that respond differently depending on the method.
214:  * The correct way to do this is to use {@link #path(String, Handler)} and {@link Context#byMethod(Action)}.
215:  * <pre class="java">{@code
216:  * import ratpack.test.embed.EmbeddedApp;
217:  * import static org.junit.Assert.*;
218:  *
219:  * public class Example {
220:  *   public static void main(String... args) throws Exception {
221:  *     EmbeddedApp.fromHandlers(c -> c
222:  *       .path("foo", ctx ->
223:  *         ctx.byMethod(m -> m
224:  *           .get(() -> ctx.render("GET"))
225:  *           .post(() -> ctx.render("POST"))
226:  *         )
227:  *       )
228:  *     ).test(httpClient -> {
229:  *       assertEquals("GET", httpClient.getText("foo"));
230:  *       assertEquals("POST", httpClient.postText("foo"));
231:  *       assertEquals(405, httpClient.delete("foo").getStatusCode());
232:  *     });
233:  *   }
234:  * }
235:  * }</pre>
236:  * <p>
237:  * Given the following, a POST to /foo will yield a 405 response.
238:  * <pre class="java">{@code
239:  * import ratpack.test.embed.EmbeddedApp;
240:  * import static org.junit.Assert.assertEquals;
241:  *
242:  * public class Example {
243:  *   public static void main(String... args) throws Exception {
244:  *     EmbeddedApp.fromHandlers(c -> c
245:  *       .get("foo", ctx -> ctx.render("GET"))
246:  *       .post("foo", ctx -> ctx.render("POST"))
247:  *     ).test(httpClient -> {
248:  *       assertEquals("GET", httpClient.getText("foo"));
249:  *
250:  *       // NOTE: returns 405, not 200 and "POST"
251:  *       assertEquals(405, httpClient.post("foo").getStatusCode());
252:  *     });
253:  *   }
254:  * }
255:  * }</pre>
256:  * <p>
257:  * All methods that match HTTP methods, are synonyms for {@link #path(String, Class)} in terms of path binding.
258:  * That is, {@link #get(Handler)} behaves the same way with regard to path binding as {@link #path(Handler)}, and not {@link #all(Handler)}.
259:  */
260: public interface Chain {
